{
  "slug": "zalcano",
  "note": "Zalcano parses structurally (its 20 main-table weights reconcile to 36), but every mechanic that decides WHO gets what lives in prose the parser does not read. From ==Drops== (revid 15287396): \"Players must do at least 5 damage to Zalcano's shield to be eligible for drops, and 31 combined damage to be eligible for uniques and pet\" — the two levelAtLeast gates below, the second reading the derived totalDamage field. The gates chain rather than alternate: uniques require BOTH >= 5 shield damage and >= 31 combined, since the 23 May 2024 Changes entry states \"All players must now deal some 'armour' damage to Zalcano to be eligible for drops\". From ===100===, citing Mod Husky: crystal shards are a role-keyed tier (1 drop-eligible, 2 unique-eligible, 3 MVP), not a 1-3 range roll, and \"Infernal ashes are only dropped for the MVP\". From ==Drops==: \"The MVP ... will also receive an additional 10% (rounded up) of their non-unique loot\" — qtyMultiplier zalcano_mvp_share with ceilDelta, applied to the main table only. The MVP bonus is deliberately NOT applied to the crystal shards table: shards are already role-tiered (the MVP gets 3), so scaling them again would double-count the same bonus. Tertiary rates are the page's own pre-multiplied figures (1/200 x 39/40 = 39/8000 tool seed, 1/200 x 1/40 = 1/8000 onyx).  TWO CURVES REMAIN UNKNOWN AND ARE NOT MODELLED, which is why zalcano stays on the mechanics watchlist. (1) The page states main- and tertiary-table drops \"scale with the player's points\" and defines the points exactly (P_M = H + 2S; P_T = min(H,400) + 2*min(S,300)), but never states the function turning points into loot — so zalcano_points stays a stub and the main table's quantities are the parsed crowd-sourced ranges. (2) The Zalcano shard is \"Between 1/750 and 1/1500 depending on contribution\" (Mod Ed, 11 May 2020) with no stated interpolation; the wiki's own primary figure (1/750) is kept rather than inventing a curve between the endpoints. Both are recorded rather than guessed, matching Duke Sucellus' frozen-tablet curve.",
